cartridge "WIZARD TAG/BOB BLACKMER/1987"

' WIZARD TAG
' A ColecoVision game based on "Wizard Tag" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy! March
' 1987 (Commodore 64, machine code at $C000 with a BASIC loader). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/wizard-ml.dis, and watched running), the game is
' rebuilt for the ColecoVision with tile graphics, layered sprites and
' sound effects.
'
' The game: six floors of a castle joined by ladders. Three keys lie on the
' floors (one always on the top floor). A creature paces each of floors 1,
' 3 and 5 (counted from the top), turning at the walls. Collect the three
' keys and the wizard appears: tag him to finish the level. One touch of a
' creature ends the game. Every level the keys are scattered again and the
' creatures move faster.
'
' From the original (orig/): six floors; ladders between them in the
' middle (floors 1-2, 3-4, 5-6) and near both ends (2-3, 4-5); walking only
' on a floor, climbing only at a ladder; the hero starts at the bottom
' left; three keys, the first on the top floor and two on random floors 2-6,
' never overlapping; three wide creatures on floors 1, 3, 5 starting at the
' right, left, right; each creature goes on in its direction and turns at
' the ends; it steps a pixel only when a random byte passes a threshold
' (about 1 in 10 for the top one, 2 in 9 for the others), and it gets one
' more such try per loop each level; a creature's touch is the end (no
' lives); keys re-scattered on each new level, hero and creatures stay where
' they are; LEVEL counter.
'
' Liberties: speeds are per sixtieth of a second (the C64's loop ran about
' 38 times a second; the hero walks a pixel a frame and climbs about 0.6, a
' little slower than the C64's 76 and 38 pixels a second, for a screen of
' 208 walking pixels); the creature's tries per frame are the level's
' number, at most 8 (from level 8 on the speed stays the same; the C64 also
' shortened its delay loop every level and wrapped it at level 21); the
' wizard: on the C64 enabling sprite 7 showed him wherever that sprite
' happened to be (its position is never set), so here he appears at a random
' place on a floor with no creature and away from the hero; a side button
' pauses; the caught hero blinks at 3 Hz; screen redrawn for the 32-column
' screen; a best level is kept on the title; title tune and sounds are new.
'
' Controls: joystick only. Left and right walk along a floor, up and down
' climb a ladder (stand at it), a side button pauses. Either controller.
' On the title, up and down choose, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.

